#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148246} :activity/id #uuid "4b9a6864-adeb-55b6-a307-9ae8a7bb1b59" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050927 :topic/id #uuid "7275db81-0f7a-5732-8201-28cd81b685b9" :topic/math-academy-id 2459} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148246 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148791 :tutorial/content "Any multiplication of fractions can also be interpreted as multiplication and division of whole numbers.\n\nFor example, consider\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{14}{\\color{blue}3} \\times \\dfrac{6}{\\color{red}7}.\n$$\n\nWriting the fractions as division, our expression becomes\n\n$$\n(14\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 3}})\\times (6\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}}) .\n$$\n\nWe use parentheses to make it easier to read.\n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can write an equivalent expression by swapping the two divisions. Swapping the ${\\color{red}{\\div\\, 3}}$ and the ${\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 7}}$, we get the following equivalent expression:\n\n$$\n(14\\, {\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}}) \\times (6\\, {\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 3}})\n$$\n\nIf we interpret the divisions as fractions again, then we have another equivalent expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{14}{\\color{red}7} \\times \\dfrac{6}{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nNotice that this amounts to *swapping the denominators* in the original expression." :tutorial/id #uuid "c473aa48-4570-5eb6-9482-3e3e9c088ea6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8589 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "eb2cdf86-6a92-5c47-ad2b-84d5074cc074" :step/math-academy-id 135500 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148247}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148247 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147404 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147168 :question/id #uuid "ba1bbec5-0ff2-52ec-ac2e-876a8ea4c70e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13755"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e0f80f6-920a-5eb0-919c-62c8ba67a2b9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050475}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430669}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Multiplication and Division as Fraction Multiplication"} :step/id #uuid "ac10ae6e-8027-5c0a-a3ee-255e0e11895c" :step/math-academy-id 135501 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148248}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148248 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147405 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147169 :question/id #uuid "ebdd2229-6712-561c-821b-7286460a6824" :question/math-academy-id "e-13758"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2d79438-2593-55c1-a937-c40f8fc6db08" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430671}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Expression as an Equivalent Product of Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "2bc90864-268c-52ba-b368-f50e8a0eee6d" :step/math-academy-id 135504 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148249}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148249 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147406 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147170 :question/id #uuid "830169cc-f331-5a91-81d6-374aad29e951" :question/math-academy-id "e-13761"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8b307c7-79bc-58de-ab88-b0783857371b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430658} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430666}] :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the Answer Is a Whole Number"} :step/id #uuid "5d27849a-7867-5a73-8f8d-1b379b861f6b" :step/math-academy-id 135507 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148250}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148250 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148792 :tutorial/content "When solving a fraction multiplication problem, it's often convenient to simplify the problem *before* carrying out the multiplication. \n\nFor example, let's consider the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{8} \\times \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}12}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's numerator ($\\color{red}3$) and the second fraction's denominator ($\\color{blue}12$) have a common \nfactor of $3.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by first swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}12} \\times \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the first fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3÷3}{12÷3}\\times \\frac{1}{8} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\times \\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\times \\dfrac{1}{8} = \\dfrac{1 \\times 1}{4 \\times 8} = \\dfrac{1}{32}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{8} \\times \\dfrac{1}{12} = \\dfrac{1}{32}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c36b33ff-a822-5308-95a8-990652f0aab8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8591 :tutorial/title "Evaluating Products of Fractions Using a Strategy"} :step/id #uuid "e26ba33d-782f-55f9-9be0-d0fb0d88f75b" :step/math-academy-id 135510 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148251}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148251 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147407 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147186 :question/id #uuid "36b559ef-d696-580d-813f-c80fc9bc36b3" :question/math-academy-id "e-14704"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee7ebc5-72be-53b1-86d8-27bd939a7244" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049253}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430657} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430670}] :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the Answer Is a Fraction"} :step/id #uuid "a8d8bfe8-541a-557f-9e2b-0145547a3650" :step/math-academy-id 135511}] :activity/title "Multiplying Fractions by Swapping Denominators" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147660}]}
